 ## The PR is the visible record
 The draft PR opened by `start` stays the live log of the raft. Every `./zn commit` pushes. In addition, comment on the PR (`gh pr comment`) when: a review starts (with the sha), a review file lands (verdict and findings by severity), a review is answered (the refute lines), an issue finishes, and on any `BLOCKED:` stop. Never sit on unpushed work, and never leave review files uncommitted. See zz-execute Phase 4.
 
+## Provenance
+`provenance.md` (in an issue's or a raft's folder) is the one record of what git cannot tell: rows `| when | event | actor | detail |`, no row numbers, appended and never edited (`./zn audit` checks it). The events are `approved`, `unblocked`, `confirmed`, `ranked`, `rafted` and `delivered`; `./zn record <id> unblocked|confirmed|ranked [--actor A] [--detail k=v]` publishes the first three (the tickets that make those events call it; an active item takes `unblocked` only), and the others are written by their own commands in a pull request. The **actor** is `human:<git name>` or `agent:<model> effort <level>`: `--actor`, else `ZN_ACTOR` (the dispatcher sets it), else `agent:unknown effort <CLAUDE_EFFORT>` when `CLAUDECODE`, `AI_AGENT` or `ZN_DISPATCH` (a dispatched step) is set and non-empty, else the git name. A published append is built from `origin/main`'s file. Two writers (a published row on `main`, a row in your PR) are united by the engine's own merges of `origin/main` (`./zn sync`, `land`, `fold`/`recombine`): never merge `origin/main` with plain `git merge`, which stops on the record. A record added in a folder `origin/main` moved (`ready`, `done`) follows the folder; any other file added there stops the merge, naming it. A sync stops too when a side edited or removed an old row. `remap` merges on its own and does not unite a record both sides appended: resolve that one by hand. `./zn record` publishes `unblocked`, `confirmed` and `ranked` for a backlog item, `unblocked` only for an active one, nothing for a done one.
+
 ## Spend (best effort, automatic)
 Rows are only ever appended. A row has nine columns: when, commit, +lines, -lines, minutes, attribution, phase and tokens, and a tenth, model, once any row of the file records the model that spent its tokens (`--model <id>` on `spend` and `commit`; a file with no such row keeps nine columns, and a row with no model leaves the cell empty). The phase is one of `capture`, `prep`, `design-review`, `build`, `code-review` or `deliver`.
